window.INTERVIEW_EXECUTION["swift-68"] = {"timeBudget": {"navigation": 4, "solution": 9, "coding": 26, "verification": 6}, "navigation": {"say": "The minute worth spending here is on the interval convention and on which requests the last question covers. Half-open or closed changes one comparison in four places, and whether supervision covers the whole list or only the accepted subset changes whether that part is interesting at all.", "questions": ["Are bookings half-open, so a request beginning as the room is released is compatible with it?", "Are the requests all for one room, or is capacity part of the question?", "Do the supervisory check-ins have to observe every request on the list, or only the ones the room accepted?", "Does the caller need the bookings themselves, or would counts be enough?"], "assumptions": ["Bookings are half-open in minutes from midnight, so touching requests are compatible.", "There is exactly one procedure room, which is what makes this a selection rather than a capacity question.", "Supervision covers every request on the day's list, accepted or declined, because supervision is about the list rather than the schedule.", "Requests finishing at the same minute are ordered by id, so a caller sees the same selection every run."], "edgeCases": ["an empty day, where every answer is empty rather than a failure", "requests meeting exactly at a boundary, which the half-open convention says all fit", "nested requests, where only the innermost is hosted and the outer ones still need observing", "two requests finishing at the same minute, which is where a missing tie-break shows"], "restate": "Given a day's booking requests for one procedure room, report the largest set the room can host, the requests that must therefore be declined, and the fewest minutes at which a supervisor must look in so every request on the list is observed, with what each check-in sees."}, "solution": {"pitch": "One sort key carries the whole file: order by the minute the room is released, earliest first. The exchange argument is what licenses it, and it is short enough to say aloud. Take any optimal schedule and swap its first booking for the compatible request that finishes earliest; the swap leaves at least as much of the day free to the right, so the count never drops, and repeating it turns any optimal schedule into this one. The declines are then the complement of the selection rather than a second minimisation, and the supervision question turns out to be the same number again, because on intervals the smallest observing set and the largest compatible selection coincide.", "dataStructures": ["One sorted array and one accumulating array. There is no auxiliary structure here at all, which is worth noticing given how often greedy reaches for a heap.", "A single integer of state during the sweep: the minute the room was last released.", "A set of accepted ids for the complement, so the declines are one filtering pass rather than a search per request.", "For the supervision plan, one bucket per check-in, filled by a walk over the day's requests in the same finish order."], "types": ["A booking is a struct with a readable string id, because every answer here is a list of requests a human then goes and looks at.", "The duration property exists for the reader rather than the algorithm, which never needs it; the sort key is the end alone.", "A check-in is its own type carrying the minute and the requests it observes, because a supervisor needs both and recovering the second afterwards is a second pass.", "The failures are a typed enum naming the offending request, since the caller's next move is to go and fix that row."], "composition": "Everything reaches the day through the ordering, so the key is defined once. The count is the selection's size rather than a second sweep, and the declines are the selection's complement. The fourth part is the one worth defending: its minutes come from the selection, because the exchange argument that justifies the selection also proves those minutes observe every request, including the declined ones. That is a composition an assertion can catch being wrong, since the check-in count must equal the compatible count on every input.", "tradeoff": "Making the selection rather than the count the primitive costs an allocation on a call that only wanted a number. I want it because two of the four parts need the bookings themselves, and a count-returning primitive would have forced the sweep to be written twice. If a hot path ever needed the count alone, that is a specialised overload, not a reason to weaken the primitive.", "trap": "Two. The first is the boundary: with half-open bookings the sweep tests `start >= lastEnd`, and the `>` version silently rejects every back-to-back pair. The second is the sort key itself, which is the whole problem in disguise. Start time and shortest duration both produce plausible schedules and both are wrong on three-request days, and a candidate who cannot state the exchange argument has no way to tell which one they wrote."}, "coding": {"order": ["Write the overlap predicate and the ordering together, since the second is the only place validation lives.", "Say the exchange argument out loud, then write the sweep it justifies.", "Check the sweep against a day with one long request containing two short disjoint ones, which is where a start-time key goes visibly wrong.", "Write the declines as a complement, in one line, and resist the urge to make them interesting.", "Argue the equality between check-ins and the selection before writing the fourth part, because the argument is the implementation.", "Finish with the attribution, which is the only genuinely new loop in that part."], "commentary": ["I am sorting by finish time, and here is why that is safe rather than convenient.", "Take any optimal schedule. Swap its first booking for the compatible request that finishes earliest. That leaves at least as much of the day free afterwards, so the count cannot drop.", "Repeat the swap and any optimal schedule turns into this one, so the greedy is optimal rather than merely reasonable.", "The bookings are half-open, so a request starting exactly when the room is released is compatible; that makes the test `>=` and not `>`.", "The declines are the complement. Fewest declines and most hosted are the same answer, so writing a second greedy here would be solving the problem twice.", "For the supervision, notice the count first: it is the same number as the selection, and that is not a coincidence on intervals.", "Every declined request was declined because it starts before some accepted request is released while ending no earlier, so that accepted request's last minute lies inside it.", "So the minutes come from the selection, and the only new work is filing each request under the earliest check-in that falls inside it."], "checkIn": "Before the fourth part I would say: I am reading the supervision question as covering every request on the day's list, not only the ones the room accepted. If it were the accepted subset the answer would be one check-in per accepted request and the question would be trivial, so I am assuming you meant the list. Tell me if not."}, "verification": {"walkthroughs": ["Walk the four worked requests: 540 to 600, 560 to 640, 600 to 660 and 630 to 700. In finish order they are already b1, b2, b3, b4.", "The sweep takes b1, skips b2 because 560 is below 600, takes b3 because 600 is not below 600, and skips b4 because 630 is below 660. Two hosted, two declined.", "Run the same day with a start-time key on a three-request example instead: a request from 0 to 100 with two short ones at 10 to 20 and 30 to 40 gives one where the right key gives two.", "Check the check-ins: 599 and 659. b2 runs from 560 to 640 and contains 599, b4 runs from 630 to 700 and contains 659, so both declined requests are observed.", "Count them: two check-ins for two hosted requests, which is the equality the fourth part rests on, and it holds on every fixture worth trying."], "tests": "Run the selector's suite and read the four parts in order. The first suite's tied-finish fixture is what catches a missing tie-break, and the boundary test is what catches the wrong comparison. The second suite checks the sweep against an exhaustive search over every subset, which is the strongest statement available here. The fourth suite asserts that the check-in count equals the compatible count on every fixture and that every request is filed exactly once.", "complexity": "The sort dominates at O(n log n), and every part after it is one linear pass with constant extra state beyond the output. The attribution in the last part looks quadratic as written and is not in practice, because each request stops at the first check-in that contains it; if the check-in list were large enough for that to matter, a merge walk over two already-sorted sequences replaces it without changing the answer.", "moreTime": "Weighted requests, where each booking carries a value and the objective is the largest total rather than the largest count. That is the moment greedy stops working and dynamic programming takes over, and knowing where that line is matters more than any of this code. Then several rooms, which turns the selection into an assignment. Then a rolling day, where requests arrive during the sweep and the exchange argument has to be re-examined against decisions already communicated to patients."}, "code": "public struct Booking: Equatable, Sendable {\n    public let id: String\n    public let start: Int\n    public let end: Int\n\n    public init(id: String, start: Int, end: Int) {\n        self.id = id\n        self.start = start\n        self.end = end\n    }\n\n    /// Bookings are half-open in minutes from midnight, so a booking that ends\n    /// where another begins occupies no shared minute of the room.\n    public var duration: Int { end - start }\n}\n\npublic struct SupervisionCheckIn: Equatable, Sendable {\n    public let minute: Int\n    public let bookingIDs: [String]\n\n    public init(minute: Int, bookingIDs: [String]) {\n        self.minute = minute\n        self.bookingIDs = bookingIDs\n    }\n}\n\npublic enum BookingError: Error, Equatable, Sendable {\n    case endNotAfterStart(id: String)\n    case duplicateBookingID(String)\n    case tooManyBookings(Int)\n    case notImplemented\n}\n\npublic struct RoomBookingSelector: Sendable {\n    /// The day's request list is bounded so the sort is the only cost that\n    /// matters and no caller can hand over an unbounded schedule.\n    public static let maximumBookingCount = 100_000\n\n    public init() {}\n\n    // MARK: Part 1 - Validate and order\n\n    /// Whether two bookings would need the room at the same minute.\n    ///\n    /// Half-open: a booking running to 600 and one starting at 600 share no\n    /// minute, so the room can host both back to back.\n    public func overlaps(_ first: Booking, _ second: Booking) -> Bool {\n        first.start < second.end && second.start < first.end\n    }\n\n    /// The requests ordered by the minute they release the room, earliest\n    /// first, with ties broken by id.\n    ///\n    /// This is the only sort key in the file, and every later part reads the\n    /// day through it. The tie-break is not decoration: two requests that end\n    /// at the same minute must order the same way on every run, or the\n    /// selection a caller sees is not reproducible.\n    public func orderedByFinish(_ bookings: [Booking]) throws(BookingError) -> [Booking] {\n        try validate(bookings)\n        return bookings.sorted { left, right in\n            left.end == right.end ? left.id < right.id : left.end < right.end\n        }\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Part 2 - The most compatible bookings\n\n    /// The largest set of requests the one room can host, as the bookings\n    /// themselves.\n    ///\n    /// The exchange argument in one sentence: take any optimal schedule and\n    /// swap its first booking for the compatible one that finishes earliest;\n    /// the swap leaves at least as much of the day free to the right, so the\n    /// count never drops, and repeating it turns any optimal schedule into\n    /// this one. That sentence is the whole justification for the key, which\n    /// is why sorting by start or by duration is not a matter of taste.\n    ///\n    /// This method, not the count, is the primitive: a count alone would force\n    /// the next part to run the sweep a second time.\n    public func selectCompatible(_ bookings: [Booking]) throws(BookingError) -> [Booking] {\n        var accepted: [Booking] = []\n        for booking in try orderedByFinish(bookings) {\n            // Half-open again: a request may start exactly when the room is\n            // released, so the test is `>=` rather than `>`.\n            guard let last = accepted.last else {\n                accepted.append(booking)\n                continue\n            }\n            if booking.start >= last.end {\n                accepted.append(booking)\n            }\n        }\n        return accepted\n    }\n\n    /// How many requests the room can host, which is a projection of the\n    /// selection rather than a second sweep.\n    public func maximumCompatibleCount(_ bookings: [Booking]) throws(BookingError) -> Int {\n        try selectCompatible(bookings).count\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Part 3 - Which requests to decline\n\n    /// The requests scheduling has to turn away, in the same finish order the\n    /// selection was made in.\n    ///\n    /// Fewest declines and most bookings hosted are the same answer read from\n    /// opposite ends, so this is the complement of the selection and not a\n    /// minimisation of its own.\n    public func bookingsToDecline(_ bookings: [Booking]) throws(BookingError) -> [Booking] {\n        let acceptedIDs = Set(try selectCompatible(bookings).map(\\.id))\n        return try orderedByFinish(bookings).filter { !acceptedIDs.contains($0.id) }\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Part 4 - Fewest supervisory check-ins\n\n    /// The fewest minutes at which a supervisor must look in so that every\n    /// request on the day's list is in progress at one of them, with the\n    /// requests each check-in observes.\n    ///\n    /// The minutes are not a new algorithm and it would be a mistake to write\n    /// one. The fewest check-ins is exactly the number of requests the room\n    /// could host, and the last minute of each accepted request is a check-in\n    /// that works: a request was declined only because it begins before some\n    /// accepted request is released while finishing no earlier than that\n    /// request does, so that accepted request's last minute lies inside it.\n    /// A maximisation and a minimisation coinciding is a property of\n    /// intervals, and it is the reason this part composes with Part 2 rather\n    /// than repeating it.\n    ///\n    /// The attribution is the work. Every request on the day's list is filed\n    /// under the earliest check-in that falls inside it, which needs a walk\n    /// over the requests rather than over the selection.\n    public func supervisionPlan(_ bookings: [Booking]) throws(BookingError) -> [SupervisionCheckIn] {\n        let minutes = try selectCompatible(bookings).map { $0.end - 1 }\n        guard !minutes.isEmpty else { return [] }\n\n        var observed: [[String]] = Array(repeating: [], count: minutes.count)\n        for booking in try orderedByFinish(bookings) {\n            // The minutes are ascending, so the first one at or after this\n            // request's start is the earliest that can fall inside it, and the\n            // argument above guarantees one exists.\n            for (index, minute) in minutes.enumerated() where minute >= booking.start {\n                observed[index].append(booking.id)\n                break\n            }\n        }\n\n        return zip(minutes, observed).map { minute, ids in\n            SupervisionCheckIn(minute: minute, bookingIDs: ids.sorted())\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// The check-in minutes alone, which is a projection of the plan.\n    public func supervisionCheckIns(_ bookings: [Booking]) throws(BookingError) -> [Int] {\n        try supervisionPlan(bookings).map(\\.minute)\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Shared validation\n\n    /// The precondition every part rests on. All four entry points reach the\n    /// day's list through `orderedByFinish`, so the check lives once, here.\n    private func validate(_ bookings: [Booking]) throws(BookingError) {\n        guard bookings.count <= Self.maximumBookingCount else {\n            throw .tooManyBookings(bookings.count)\n        }\n        var seen = Set<String>()\n        seen.reserveCapacity(bookings.count)\n        for booking in bookings {\n            guard booking.end > booking.start else {\n                throw .endNotAfterStart(id: booking.id)\n            }\n            guard seen.insert(booking.id).inserted else {\n                throw .duplicateBookingID(booking.id)\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n"};
